tion of \\(2\\) removes the double count of the two lines\n\\(y=1\\) (which also belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) when \\(m=2\\)) and\n\\(x=1\\) (which also belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) when \\(m=2\\)).  \nThus we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are non‑sunny.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=1+b\\in\\mathcal D\\).  \nIf \\(b=1\\) the same argument applies with the diagonal line \\(x+y=1+a\\).  \nIf \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \nConsequently either \\(a\\le n-2\\) (so the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\)) or \\(b\\le n-2\\) (so it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\)).  \nIn every case the point belongs to at least one line of the collection.  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) satisfy the required covering condition and contain **no** sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Introducing sunny lines)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFrom Lemma 1 we start with the covering \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) consisting of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nFor each integer \\(m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le n\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nL_m:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=m\\}\n\\]\n\nlies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=m\\).  The points of \\(L_m\\) are collinear, and the line \\(x+y=m\\) has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct values \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) from \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor each \\(j\\) we replace the diagonal line \\(x+y=m_j\\) by a new line \\(\\ell_j\\) defined as follows.\n\nSelect a point \\(P_j\\in L_{m_j}\\) (for instance the leftmost point of \\(L_{m_j}\\)).  \nLet \\(\\ell_j\\) be the unique line passing through \\(P_j\\) with a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); for concreteness we may take sl