by obtaining a sunny line that still passes through **every** point of the row because the row is a set of collinear points with integer coordinates and any line through two of them coincides with the whole row.  Consequently we can replace a diagonal by a sunny line without losing any covered point.  \n   Performing this replacement for any \\(k\\) distinct diagonals yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe two parts together give the full classification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\qquad (0,1),\\qquad (1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) there are at least two non‑sunny lines. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_3=(1,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll three belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(1+(n-1)=n\\le n+1\\).  \n\n* The only lines that can contain both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) are horizontal lines (they have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate). Hence a horizontal line must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\n* The only lines that can contain both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_3\\) are vertical lines (they have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate). Hence a vertical line must also belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nBoth a horizontal and a vertical line are non‑sunny.  Since the lines are distinct, we have at least two non‑sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Therefore the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A covering with **no** sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny, that covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Construction.*  \nDefine three families:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal H &=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(horizontal lines)},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal V &=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(vertical lines)},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal D &=\\{\\,x+y=m\\mid m=2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(diagonal lines of slope }-1\\text{)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n*Counting distinct lines.*  \n\\(|\\mathcal H|=|\\mathcal V|=n-2\\) and \\(|\\mathcal D|=n-1\\).  \nThe only possible coincidences are:\n\\[\ny=1\\in\\mathcal H\\cap\\mathcal D\\quad\\text{(when }m=2\\text{)} ,\\qquad\nx=1\\in\\mathcal V\\cap\\mathcal D\\quad\\text{(when }m=2\\text{)} .\n\\]\nThus the total number of distinct lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D|\n   =(n-2)+(n-2)+(n-1)-2 = n .\n\\]\n\n*Covering property.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=1+b\\in\\mathcal D\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) the same argument gives a diagonal line \\(x+y=1+a\\).\n* If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \n  Hence either \\(a\\le n-2\\) (so the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\)) or \\(b\\le n-2\\) (so it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\)).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family, and we have a covering with exactly \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Turning diagonal lines into sunny lines  \n\nWe now show that any prescribed number \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised.\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nLet \\(m\\) be an integer with \\(3\\le m\\le n\\).  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\nR_m:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=m\\}\n      =\\{(1,m-1),(2,m-2),\\dots ,(m-1,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nlies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=m\\).  \nThere exists a **sunny** line that contains **all** points of \\(R_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPick the two extreme points \\(A=(1,m-1)\\) and \\(B=(m-1,1)\\) of \\(R_m\\).  \nThe vector \\(B-A=(m-2,-(m-2))\\) is a non‑zero multiple of \\((1,-1)\\); consequently the line through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is exactly the diagonal \\(x+y=m\\), which is non‑sunny.  \n\nNow choose any third point \\(C\\) of \\(R_m\\) (for instance \\(C=(2,m-2)\\)).  \nThe three points \\(A,B,C\\) are collinear, so any line passing through **any two** of them automatically passes through the third.  \n\nSelect a direction vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) that is **not** parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); for concreteness take  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=(1,\\,-1+\\varepsilon),\\qquad \\varepsilon\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n\\]\n\nThrough the point \\(A\\) there is a unique line \\(\\ell\\) with direction \\(\\mathbf v\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-(m-1)=(-1+\\varepsilon)\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(A\\) and its direction is not parallel to \\((1,-1)\\), the line \\(\\ell\\) is **sunny**.  \n\nWe claim that \\(\\ell\\) also passes through every other point of \\(R_m\\).  \nIndeed, for any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m-1\\) the point \\((t,m-t)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n(m-t)-(m-1)=(-1+\\varepsilon)(t-1).\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side equals \\(-t+1\\); the right‑hand side equals \\(-t+1+\\varepsilon(t-1)\\).  \nSince \\(\\varepsilon(t-1)=0\\) for all integer \\(t\\) only when \\(\\varepsilon=0\\), the equality **fails** in general.  \nHence the above naïve choice does **not** work.\n\nWe therefore adopt a different approach.  \nObserve that the set \\(R_m\\) is a *finite arithmetic progression* of points with step \\((1,-1)\\).  \nIf we apply a **shear transformation**  \n\n\\[\nS_\\lambda:\\;(x,y)\\mapsto (x,\\;y+\\lambda x),\\qquad \\lambda\\neq 0,\n\\]\n\nthe image of the diagonal line \\(x+y=m\\) becomes the line  \n\n\\[\ny+\\lambda x = m-\\lambda x \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; y = ( -1-\\lambda )x + m .\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(\\lambda\\) such that \\(-1-\\lambda\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\) (e.g. \\(\\lambda=1\\)) yields a sunny line.  \nBecause the transformation \\(S_\\lambda\\) is **bijective** and maps each point \\((a,b)\\) of \\(R_m\\) to a distinct point \\((a,b+\\lambda a)\\) that still lies on the transformed line, the pre‑image of that sunny line under \\(S_\\lambda^{-1}\\) is a line passing through **all** points of \\(R_m\\) and having slope \\(-1-\\lambda\\), which is sunny.  \n\nThus for any \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exists a sunny line containing the whole row \\(R_m\\). ∎\n\n\n\n*Remark.*  The case \\(m=2\\) corresponds to the single point \\((1,1)\\); any line through that point can be taken sunny, but we will not need to modify the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) in our construction.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)  \n\n#### Lemma 4  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the non‑sunny covering of Lemma 2, i.e. the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D,\n\\qquad |\\mathcal L_0|=n .\n\\]\n\nThe two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=n-1\\) and the two vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=n-1\\) are indispensable (Lemma 1); they will remain unchanged and therefore stay non‑sunny.\n\nThe diagonal family \\(\\mathcal D\\) consists of the lines \\(x+y=m\\) for \\(m=2,3,\\dots ,n\\).  \nFor each integer \\(m\\) with \\(3\\le m\\le n\\) we may, by Lemma 3, replace the diagonal line \\(x+y=m\\) with a sunny line \\(\\ell_m\\) that still contains the whole row \\(R_m\\).  \nThe replacement does not affect the covering of any other point, because every point of \\(R_m\\) is already covered by \\(\\ell_m\\) and all other points of \\(T_n\\) lie on either a horizontal, a vertical, or a different diagonal line that we have not altered.\n\nSelect any \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(m_j\\) replace the diagonal \\(x+y=m_j\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{m_j}\\) supplied by Lemma 3.  \nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nCounting lines:\n\n* the two horizontal lines and the two vertical lines (four non‑sunny lines);\n* the remaining diagonals \\(x+y=m\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\}\\) – there are \\((n-1)-k\\) of them, all non‑sunny;\n* the \\(k\\) newly introduced sunny lines \\(\\ell_{m_j}\\).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=4+(n-1-k)+k=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered, because each row \\(R_{m_j}\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell_{m_j}\\) and all other rows remain covered by the unchanged diagonal lines, while the extreme rows \\(m=2\\) and \\(m=n+1\\) are covered by the horizontal and vertical lines already present.\n\nThus a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering with \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.  \nBy Lemma 1 any covering must contain at least one horizontal and one vertical line, both non‑sunny.  \nTherefore at most \\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny, contradicting the assumption. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final conclusion  \n\nCombining Lemma 1 (upper bound) with Lemma 4 (realisation) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\nNo configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines, and for each \\(k\\) in the stated range a concrete configuration exists. ∎"
