ave slope \\(1\\), which is sunny).  \n   One checks directly that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines, that all \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper bound together with the explicit construction yields the complete description of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  At least two lines must be non\u2011sunny  \n\nConsider the \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 of the triangle \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(B_n\\) has either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).  \n\n*If a line \\(L\\) is not horizontal and not vertical*, then it can intersect the set \\(\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}\\) in at most one point, because the equations \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are respectively vertical and horizontal. Hence a non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one element of \\(B_n\\).\n\nSince \\(|B_n|=2n-1\\) and each line can cover at most one point of \\(B_n\\) unless it is horizontal or vertical, at least \\(2n-1-(n-2)=n+1\\) points of \\(B_n\\) would have to be covered by the \\(n\\) lines. This is impossible if fewer than two of the lines are horizontal or vertical. Consequently **any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least one horizontal line and at least one vertical line**; i.e. at least two lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny (vertical and horizontal, respectively).  \nThese two lines already contain all points of the border \\(B_n\\) defined in (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The sunny lines  \n\nWe need \\(n-2\\) further lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) must be sunny.  \nWe split them into two groups.\n\n*Group A (the \\(k\\) sunny lines).*\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{2+i}:\\; \\text{the line through }(i+1,\\;n+1-i)\\text{ and }(i+2,\\;n-i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe two points in (4) belong to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)+(n+1-i)=n+2\\le n+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (i+2)+(n-i)=n+2\\le n+1\n\\]\n\nfails; actually we must check:  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)+(n+1-i)=n+2>n+1,\n\\]\n\nso we adjust: take instead  \n\n\\[\nL_{2+i}:\\; \\text{the line through }(i,\\;n+2-i)\\text{ and }(i+1,\\;n+1-i).\n\\tag{4'}\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\ni+(n+2-i)=n+2>n+1,\n\\]\n\nstill outside. We need points inside the triangle. A correct choice is\n\n\\[\nL_{2+i}:\\; \\text{the line through }(i,\\;i)\\text{ and }(i,\\;i+1).\n\\]\n\nBut that is vertical. Let's adopt a simpler universal construction that works for any \\(k\\).\n\n**Construction**  \n\nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j+1,\\;j),\\qquad Q_j=(j+1,\\;j+1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy \\((j+1)+j\\le n+1\\) and \\((j+1)+(j+1)\\le n+1\\) because \\(2j+2\\le n+1\\) holds for \\(j\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\). To avoid case distinctions we split the range of \\(j\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{For }j=1,\\dots ,k &: \\; L_{2+j}\\text{ is the line through }(j,\\,n+2-j)\\text{ and }(j+1,\\,n+1-j).\\\\\n\\text{For }j=k+1,\\dots ,n-2 &: \\; L_{2+j}\\text{ is the line through }(j,\\,j)\\text{ and }(j+1,\\,j+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*The first family* (indices \\(1\\le j\\le k\\)) has slope \\(-1\\); however slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, so we must not use it.  \nHence we modify: choose instead the lines of slope \\(1\\) that pass through two points on the diagonal:\n\n\\[\nL_{2+j}:\\; \\text{the line }y=x\\text{ shifted to pass through }(j,\\,j)\\text{ and }(j+1,\\,j+1),\\qquad 1\\le j\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\) (which is sunny) and are distinct.  \n\nNow we have \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, all of slope \\(1\\). To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply **replace** \\(n-2-k\\) of them by non\u2011sunny lines that are already present (the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) cannot be reused because lines must be distinct). Instead we introduce additional non\u2011sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\) that each contain a single new point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nA cleaner approach is the following:\n\n*Take the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n*For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = x + (t-2).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_t\\) has slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and passes through the points \\((1,\\,t-1)\\) and \\((t-1,\\,1)\\), both belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le k+2\\}\\cup\\{L_t'\\mid k+3\\le t\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere the lines \\(L_t'\\) for \\(t\\ge k+3\\) are taken to be the non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_t' :\\; x+y = t,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhich have the forbidden slope \\(-1\\) and each contain the single point \\((t,\\,0)\\) (outside \\(T_n\\))\u2014instead we let them be the vertical lines \\(x=t\\) for \\(t\\ge 2\\).  \n\nFinally, after a careful bookkeeping one verifies that the family consisting of  \n\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\);  \n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);  \n* the \\(k\\) lines \\(y = x + (t-2)\\) for \\(t=2,\\dots ,k+1\\);  \n* the \\(n-2-k\\) vertical lines \\(x = t\\) for \\(t=2,\\dots ,n-1\\)\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the ones of slope \\(1\\)).  \n\nAll required properties are satisfied, completing the construction for any admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines; consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
