nes\n   must be non\u2011sunny.\n\n5. **Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Take the following \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   - the horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots , y=n-2\\);\n   - the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n   These \\(n-2\\) lines are distinct and each of them belongs to the\n   non\u2011sunny family.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   The points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not yet covered are exactly the\n   \\((k+2)\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,\\;(k+1,n-k),\\;(k+2,n-k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   (All points with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge n-1\\) are missed by the chosen\n   non\u2011sunny lines, and there are precisely \\(k+2\\) of them because\n   \\(k\\le n-2\\).)\n\n   For each of the first \\(k\\) of these points we draw a distinct sunny\n   line passing through it and through the point \\((n,1)\\).  The line\n   through \\((a,b)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle\n   \\frac{1-b}{\\,n-a\\,}\\); since \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n-1\\) this slope is\n   different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so the line is sunny.  The \\(k\\) lines are\n   distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n   *The last two uncovered points.*  \n   The remaining two points \\((k+1,n-k)\\) and \\((k+2,n-k-1)\\) lie on the\n   non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)).  Adding this line\n   gives a total of  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)\\;+\\;k\\;+\\;1\\;=\\;n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   One checks directly that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at\n   least one of the constructed lines, completing the covering.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) and the explicit construction for\n   each admissible \\(k\\) together prove that the set of possible numbers of\n   sunny lines is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\n(horizontal or vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\nOtherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nPoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on it are \\((a,c)\\) where\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such values of \\(a\\).\nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\) by symmetry.\nFor a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\),\nthe admissible points are \\((a,t-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le t-1\\); again at most\n\\(n\\) possibilities. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Because its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(L\\) meets each\nhorizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) in at most one point; the same is\ntrue for each vertical line \\(x=c\\) and each line \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le\nn+1\\)).  Since \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of these three families, among\nthe \\(n\\) possible values of \\(c\\) (or \\(t\\)) exactly one is missed:\nif \\(L\\) were to intersect all \\(n\\) horizontals, its slope would be \\(0\\);\nif it intersected all \\(n\\) verticals, its slope would be \\(\\infty\\);\nif it intersected all \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=t\\), its slope would be \\(-1\\).\nHence at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lie on \\(L\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Necessary condition\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and that exactly\n\\(k\\) of them are sunny.  By Lemma\u202f2 the sunny lines together cover at most\n\\(k(n-1)\\) points; the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover at most\n\\((n-k)n\\) points (Lemma\u202f1).  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk(n-1)+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nExpanding the left\u2013hand side gives \\(kn-nk+n^2-n\\), i.e. \\(n^2-n\\).  Inequality\n(1) becomes\n\n\\[\nn^2-n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n2n-2\\;\\ge\\;n+1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\)**; in particular at\nleast two lines must be non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-2\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-2,\\qquad x=1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Points still uncovered after (2).**  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) minus the points lying on the lines (2) consists\n   of the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,\\;(k+1,n-k),\\;(k+2,n-k-1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge n-1\\) fails to satisfy\n   \\(y=b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and \\(x=a\\neq 1\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\n   forces \\(a\\le k+2\\), giving exactly the list (3).  Because \\(k\\le n-2\\),\n   the list contains \\(k+2\\) points.\n\n3. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each of the first \\(k\\) points in (3) we draw the line through that\n   point and the fixed point \\((n,1)\\).  The line through \\((a,b)\\) and\n   \\((n,1)\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   y-1=\\frac{1-b}{\\,n-a\\,}(x-n).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n-1\\), the slope \\(\\frac{1-b}{n-a}\\) is\n   different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each of these\n   \\(k\\) lines i