  construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is\n   attainable.  Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then the\ncondition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) integer\nvalues of \\(a\\) are possible.  The same bound holds for a vertical line\n\\(x=c\\).  If the line has slope \\(-1\\) it can be written \\(x+y=t\\) with\n\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\); then \\(a\\) can range from \\(1\\) to \\(t-1\\), again at most\n\\(n\\) possibilities. ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a sunny line \\(L\\).  Because its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(L\\) meets each\nhorizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\)) in at most one point; similarly it\nmeets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) and each diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\)\n(\\(t=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)) in at most one point.  Since \\(L\\) is not parallel to any\nof these three families, among the \\(n\\) possible values of the constant at\nleast one value is missed: if \\(L\\) intersected all \\(n\\) horizontals it would\nbe horizontal, contradicting sunniness; the same reasoning applies to the\nvertical and diagonal families.  Consequently \\(L\\) can contain points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belonging to at most \\(n-1\\) distinct values of the constant,\nhence at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Necessary condition\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and that exactly\n\\(k\\) of them are sunny.  By Lemma 2 the sunny lines together cover at most\n\\(k(n-1)\\) points; the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines cover at most\n\\((n-k)n\\) points (Lemma 1).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nk(n-1)+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{★}\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side simplifies:\n\n\\[\nk(n-1)+(n-k)n = kn-k + n^{2}-kn = n^{2}-k .\n\\]\n\nThus (★) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\); at least two of\nthe \\(n\\) lines are necessarily non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficiency: construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 1. Non‑sunny lines\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{0}:x=1,\\qquad\nL_{i}:y=i\\;(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-2\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n#### 2. Points not covered by the lines above\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) fails to lie on any of the lines\n\\(L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\) exactly when \\(a\\ge 2\\) (so it is not on\n\\(x=1\\)) and \\(b\\ge n-1\\) (so it is not on any of the horizontals\n\\(y=1,\\dots ,n-2\\)).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then forces\n\\(a\\le k+2\\).  Consequently the uncovered points are precisely  \n\n\\[\nQ_{0}=(1,n),\\;\nQ_{1}=(2,n-1),\\;\n\\ldots ,\\;\nQ_{k}=(k+1,n-k),\\;\nQ_{k+1}=(k+2,n-k-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(k+2\\) such points because \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 3. Sunny lines covering the first \\(k\\) of them\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let \\(S_{i}\\) be the line through \\(Q_{i}\\) and the\nfixed point \\(R:=(n,1)\\).  The slope of \\(S_{i}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{1-(n-i)}{\\,n-i\\,}= 