he family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\}\\cup\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\cup\\{D\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains \\((n-2)+k+1=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n   By construction every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line\n   of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (the points \\(Q_{i}\\) are covered either by a sunny line\n   \\(S_{i}\\) or by the diagonal \\(D\\); all other points lie on one of the\n   horizontal or the vertical line of (2)).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); the explicit\n   construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is\n   attainable.  Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then from\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n\\) integer values of\n\\(a\\) are possible.  The same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and for a\nline of slope \\(-1\\), which can be written \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\);\nagain \\(a\\) (or \\(b\\)) can take at most \\(n\\) values. ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nConsider the three families of lines that define the boundary of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\):\n\n* horizontals \\(\\;y=c\\;(c=1,\\dots ,n)\\);\n* verticals \\(\\;x=c\\;(c=1,\\dots ,n)\\);\n* diagonals \\(\\;x+y=t\\;(t=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\).\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of these families, hence it meets each\nfamily in exactly one line of the family.  Consequently a sunny line can meet\nat most one line of each family at a point belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nIf the sunny line met *all* \\(n\\) horizontals at points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\),\nits equation would be \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(b\\) taking the \\(n\\) distinct values\n\\(1-m\\cdot 1,\\dots ,1-m\\cdot n\\); this would force \\(m=0\\), contradicting\nthe definition of sunny.  Hence at least one horizontal line of the family\ncontains no point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the sunny line.  The same reasoning\napplies to the vertical and diagonal families.  Since the three families\ntogether contain exactly \\(3n\\) lines but the sunny line can intersect at most\n\\(2n\\) of them (it misses at least one from each family), the total number of\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the sunny line is at most \\(n-1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Necessary condition\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\)\nof them are sunny.  By Lemma 2 the sunny lines together cover at most\n\\(k(n-1)\\) points; the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines cover at most\n\\((n-k)n\\) points (Lemma 1).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nk(n-1)+(n-k)n\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side equals \\(n^{2}-k\\); inequality (1) becomes  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\); the cases\n\\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) are impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficiency\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   \\[\n   L_{0}:x=1,\\qquad\n   L_{i}:y=i\\;(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Points not on the lines (3).**  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) is not on any line of (3) iff\n   \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge n-1\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then forces\n   \\(a\\le k+2\\).  Hence the set of uncovered points is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_{0}=(1,n),\\;\n   Q_{1}=(2,n-1),\\;\n   \\dots ,\\;\n   Q_{k}=(k+1,n-k),\\;\n   Q_{k+1}=(k+2,n-k-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (There are \\(k+2\\) points because \\(k\\le n-2\\).)\n\n3. **Sunny lines covering \\(Q_{1},\\dots ,Q_{k}\\).**  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let \\(S_{i}\\) be the line through \\(Q_{i}\\) and\n   the point \\(R:=(n,1)\\).  Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{i}= \\frac{1-(n-i)}{\\,n-i\\,}= -\\frac{n-i-1}{\\,n-i\\,},\n   \\]\n\n   which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore each\n   \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  Because the slopes \\(m_{i}\\) are distinct, the lines\n   \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\n4. **Covering the remaining two points.**  \n   The points \\(Q_{0}\\) and \\(Q_{k+1}\\) satisfy \\(x+y=n+1\\); they lie on the\n   diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   whose slope is \\(-1\\); thus \\(D\\) is non‑sunny.\n\n5. **The whole family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\}\\cup\n   \\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\cup\\{D\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\((n-2)+k+1=n\\) distinct lines.\n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are sunny.\n   By construction every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line\n   of \\(\\mathcal L\\): the points listed in (4) are covered either by a sunny\n   line \\(S_{i}\\) or by the diagonal \\(D\\); all other points lie on one of the\n   horizontal or the vertical line of (3).\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) (derived from Lemmas 1 and 2) and the\nexplicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\) together prove that the set of\npossible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nHence the problem is completely solved."
