            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then from\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n\\) integer values of\n\\(a\\) are possible.  The same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and for a\nline of slope \\(-1\\), which can be written \\(x+y=t\\) with\n\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\); again at most \\(n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) (or \\(b\\))\nare possible. ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nAssume, for contradiction, that it contains \\(n\\) distinct points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Then the set of their \\(x\\)-coordinates must be the whole set\n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (otherwise we would have fewer than \\(n\\) points).  For\neach such \\(x\\) the corresponding \\(y\\) is uniquely determined, so the set of\nsums \\(x+y\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct integers taken from the set\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), which also has size \\(n\\).  Consequently the sums are\nexactly \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  In particular the extreme sums \\(2\\) and\n\\(n+1\\) occur, giving the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) on the line.  Their\ndifference yields slope\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-n}{\\,n-1\\,}=-1,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the assumption that the line is sunny.  Hence a sunny line can\ncontain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Necessary condition\n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\nand contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  By Lemma 2 the sunny lines together\ncover at most \\(k(n-1)\\) points; the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines cover at\nmost \\((n-k)n\\) points (Lemma 1).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nk(n-1)+(n-k)n\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe left–hand side simplifies to \\(n^{2}-k\\); inequality (4) becomes  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\); the cases\n\\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) are impossible.\n\n\n\n### Sufficiency\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   \\[\n   L_{0}:x=1,\\qquad\n   L_{i}:y=i\\;(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   These are \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines (one vertical, the others\n   horizontal).\n\n2. **Uncovered points.**  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) is not on any line of (6) exactly when\n   \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n   \\(a\\le k+1\\).  Hence the uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_{i}=(i+1,\\;n-i)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   There are precisely \\(k\\) such points.\n\n3. **Sunny lines covering them.**  \n   Let \\(P:=(n,0)\\); note that \\(P\\) does not lie on any line of (6).  For each\n   \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define \\(S_{i}\\) as the line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q_{i}\\).\n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{i}= \\frac{0-(n-i)}{\\,n-(i+1)}=\n          -\\frac{\\,n-i\\,}{\\,n-i-1\\,},\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never \\(-1\\); therefore each\n   \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  Because the fractions \\(\\frac{n-i}{n-i-1}\\) are distinct,\n   the slopes \\(m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,\n   S_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\n4. **The whole family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-k-1}\\}\\cup\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The first block contains \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, the second block contains\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines; consequently \\(|\\mathcal L|=(n-k)+k=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\)\n   of them are sunny.\n\n   By construction every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line\n   of \\(\\mathcal L\\): the points listed in (7) lie on the sunny lines\n   \\(S_{i}\\); all other points lie on one of the horizontal or the vertical\n   line of (6).\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nHence a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can have\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines if and only if \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
