nd the construction shows that\n   every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  Hence the set of\n   admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the points with \\(a+b=s\\) form a\ndiagonal of length \\(s-1\\); summing these lengths gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then from\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n\\) integer values of\n\\(a\\) are possible.  The same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and for a\nline of slope \\(-1\\), which can be written \\(x+y=t\\) with\n\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\); again at most \\(n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) (or \\(b\\))\nare possible. \u220e\n\n\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 sunny lines\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).\nAssume that it contains \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nThen the set of their \\(x\\)-coordinates must be the whole set\n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); otherwise we would have fewer than \\(n\\) points.\nConsequently the set of sums \\(x+y\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct integers taken\nfrom \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), which also has size \\(n\\).  Hence the sums are\nexactly \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  In particular the extreme sums \\(2\\) and\n\\(n+1\\) occur, giving the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) on the line.\nTheir difference yields slope \\(\\dfrac{1-n}{\\,n-1\\,}=-1\\), contradicting the\nassumption that the line is sunny.  Therefore a sunny line can contain at\nmost \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### 4.  A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers at most \\(n-1\\) points, and from\nLemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence the total\nnumber of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk(n-1)+(n-k)n = n^{2}-k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause all \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nk\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInequality (3) is always satisfied for any \\(k\\le n\\); therefore it does not\nby itself forbid large values of \\(k\\).  A stronger restriction comes from the\n*directional* nature of the three families of parallel lines.\n\nConsider the three families  \n\n* vertical lines (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)),\n* horizontal lines (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)),\n* lines of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on exactly one line of each family.\nIf a line of a given family is sunny, it can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points,\nwhereas a non\u2011sunny line of that family can contain at most \\(n\\) points\n(Lemma\u202f1).  To cover all points, each family must contain **at least one**\nnon\u2011sunny line; otherwise the family would consist solely of sunny lines,\neach contributing at most \\(n-1\\) points, and the total number of points\ncovered by that family would be at most \\((n-1)n < |\\mathcal P_n|\\).\n\nThus we need at least one non\u2011sunny vertical line, at least one non\u2011sunny\nhorizontal line, and at least one non\u2011sunny line of slope \\(-1\\).  However,\nthe three required lines cannot all be distinct, because any two of them\nintersect at a point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and a third line passing through the\nsame point would coincide with one of the first two (the three directions are\npairwise distinct).  Consequently **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be\nnon\u2011sunny.  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInequality (4) is the necessary condition.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 5.1  Non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{0}:x=1,\\qquad\nL_{i}:y=i\\quad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines (one vertical, the others\nhorizontal).\n\n#### 5.2  Points not covered by (5)\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) fails to lie on any line of (5) precisely\nwhen \\(a\\ge 2\\) (so it is not on \\(x=1\\)) **and** \\(b\\ge n-k\\) (so it is not on\nany of the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k-1\\)).  From the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le k+1\\).  Hence the uncovered points are\n\n\\[\nQ_{i}=(i+1,\\;n-i)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(k\\) such points.\n\n#### 5.3  Sunny lines covering the uncovered points  \n\nLet \\(P:=(n,0)\\); note that \\(P\\) does **not** belong to any line of (5).\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define \\(S_{i}\\) as the line through \\(P\\) and\n\\(Q_{i}\\).  Its slope is\n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{0-(n-i)}{\\,n-(i+1)}=\n       -\\frac{\\,n-i\\,}{\\,n-i-1\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n-i\\ge 2\\) for all \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\), the denominator is non\u2011zero,\nso the slope is defined; it is never \\(0\\) (the numerator is non\u2011zero) and it\nis never \\(-1\\) because\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{n-i}{\\,n-i-1\\,}=-1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;n-i=n-i-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible.  Hence each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nThe function \\(f(t)=\\dfrac{t}{t-1}\\) is strictly decreasing for integers\n\\(t\\ge 2\\); therefore the numbers \\(\\frac{n-i}{n-i-1}\\) are distinct for\ndifferent \\(i\\).  Consequently the slopes \\(m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\) are distinct,\nso the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\nEach \\(S_{i}\\) contains the point \\(Q_{i}\\) from (6), so all uncovered points\nare now covered.\n\n#### 5.4  The whole family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\underbrace{\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-k-1}\\}}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny, }n-k\\text{ lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}}_{\\text{sunny, }k\\text{ lines}} .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly\n\\(k\\) of which are sunny.  By construction every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies\non at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\): the points listed in (6) are covered\nby the sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\); all remaining points belong to one of the\nhorizontal or the vertical line of (5).\n\nThus for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration\nexists.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nInequality (4) shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy\n\\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit construction of Section\u202f5 shows that every\ninteger \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed attainable.  Therefore the set\nof admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
