orces \\(c=i+1\\), so at most one point of (3) can lie on such a line.\n\nHence **any non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point of the set (3).**\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq0\\) and \\(\\neq-1\\); therefore it meets the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in exactly one point.  Consequently a sunny line can also contain at most one point of (3).\n\nThus each line of the configuration can cover at most one of the points (3).  \nSince there are \\(n\\) points, we need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines to cover them all.  Consequently at most one of the \\(n\\) lines may be non\u2011sunny; otherwise we would need more than \\(n\\) lines.\n\nIf exactly one line were non\u2011sunny, it could cover at most one point of (3), leaving at least \\(n-1\\) points to be covered by sunny lines.  As each sunny line covers at most one such point, we would need at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Adding the single non\u2011sunny line gives \\(n\\) lines, which is permissible, but it shows that **the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\)**: if there were \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, the remaining (non\u2011sunny) line would have to cover the last point of (3), which is impossible because a non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point and the remaining point would already be covered by a sunny line.  A more transparent way is to observe that the set (3) already forces at least two sunny lines (otherwise the single non\u2011sunny line would have to contain two distinct points of (3), contradicting the observation above).  Hence the maximum possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\).\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge2).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  The sunny lines\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\qquad(1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=\\alpha_i x\\qquad(1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nThese \\(k\\) lines are sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\n#### 2.2  The non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m-1\\) put  \n\n\\[\nN_{k+t}= \n\\begin{cases}\n\\{x=t\\} &\\text{if }t\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{y=t\\} &\\text{if }t\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m\\) is odd we still need one more line; we take  \n\n\\[\nN_{k+m}= \\{\\,x+y=n+2-m\\,\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\).  When \\(m\\) is even the set (5) already supplies exactly \\(m\\) lines, so (6) is omitted.\n\nAll lines defined in (5)\u2013(6) are non\u2011sunny (they are vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) and are distinct from each other and from the sunny lines \\(S_i\\).\n\nThus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that the lines cover \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); hence \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le m\\).*  \n\n- If \\(a\\) is odd, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is \\(N_{k+a}\\) by (5)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n- If \\(a\\) is even, the horizontal line \\(y=a\\) (which is \\(N_{k+a}\\) by (5)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a>m\\).*  \n\nThen \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(m+1)=n-m\\).  Because \\(m=n-k\\ge2\\), we have \\(n+2-m\\ge3\\).  Moreover\n\n\\[\na+b\\le a+(n+2-m-1)=n+2-m+(a-1)\\le n+2-m+(m-1)=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a+b=n+2-m\\) is possible, i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the line \\(x+y=n+2-m\\).  This line is precisely \\(N_{k+m}\\) defined in (6) (present when \\(m\\) is odd; when \\(m\\) is even the same equality forces \\(a+b=n+2-m\\) with \\(m\\) even, but then \\(a\\le m\\) would have held, contradicting the assumption \\(a>m\\); therefore the odd\u2011\\(m\\) case is the only one where \\(a>m\\) can occur).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of the family.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (4) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction of Section\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) the condition is also sufficient.\n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
