ven the set \\(R\\) is exhausted by the pairs \\((P_i,Q_i)\\); when \\(n\\) is odd there remains the single point \\((\\frac{n-1}{2},\\frac{n-1}{2})\\), which we cover by an additional sunny line of any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence the total number of sunny lines needed to cover \\(R\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   s=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-4, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n-3, & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Observe that \\(s\\le n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n   *Adjusting the number of sunny lines.*  \n   The construction above yields a family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \n   If the prescribed \\(k\\) satisfies \\(0\\le k\\le s\\) we simply discard \\(s-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) and replace each discarded line by a **new** sunny line that passes through the same point of \\(R\\) that was lost; this is always possible because a single point can be covered by infinitely many sunny lines.  \n   Consequently we can obtain a family with **any** number \\(k\\) of sunny lines such that \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and the explicit construction above shows that every number \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\) is attainable.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\). ∎\n\n--- \n\n**Final Answer**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
