\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny* (i.e. are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (A convenient non\u2011sunny covering)\n\nThe three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, contain altogether \\(3n\\) lines, and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one line of each family.  \nConsequently each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on three lines, none of which is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equations \\(x=a\\), \\(y=b\\) and \\(x+y=a+b\\). The three equations define respectively a vertical, a horizontal and a diagonal line, none of which is parallel to the forbidden directions. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Adding sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). There exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through at least one point of \\(T_n\\), such that the set of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by these \\(k\\) lines has cardinality at least \\(k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for definiteness we may take  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\tan\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{j\\pi}{k+1}\\Bigr),\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,k,\n\\]\n\nwhich are all admissible.  \nProceed inductively. Suppose we have already chosen sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{j-1}\\) covering a set \\(C_{j-1}\\subseteq T_n\\). Because \\(|T_n|=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2-1\\ge n\\ge k\\), there exists a point \\(P_j\\in T_n\\setminus C_{j-1}\\). The line through