up\\mathcal D|=3n\\) and, more importantly, that there are exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines among the three families with distinct constants (the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n\\) are already \\(n\\) different lines; the horizontal and diagonal families provide further lines but we never need more than \\(n\\) of them).  \n\nConsequently we can select a subfamily of \\(\\mathcal N\\) consisting of exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines (if \\(|\\mathcal N|<n-k\\) we simply add arbitrary unused lines from \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\); this does not affect the covering).  \n\nThus we obtain a total family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L = \\{ \\text{the }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}\\cup\\{\\text{the }n-k\\text{ chosen non\u2011sunny lines}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration consists of precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence \\(k\\le n\\) is necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration satisfying the two required conditions exists, and no configuration can have \\(k>n\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
