ed are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct admissible slopes, for instance  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\tan\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{j\\pi}{k+1}\\Bigr),\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,k,\n\\]\n\nso that none of them equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nConstruct the lines inductively.  \nAssume that for some \\(j\\ge 1\\) we have already chosen lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{j-1}\\) and that the set  \n\n\\[\nS_{j-1}= \\bigl\\{\\,P\\in T_n\\mid P\\text{ lies on some }L_i,\\;i<j\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains fewer than \\(j\\) points.  \nBecause \\(|T_n|=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2-1\\ge n\\ge k\\), the set \\(T_n\\setminus S_{j-1}\\) is non‑empty; pick any point \\(P_j\\) from it.  \nThe line through \\(P_j\\) with slope \\(m_j\\) is distinct from the previously drawn lines (different slope) and, by construction, is sunny.  \nDefine \\(L_j\\) to be this line and set \\(S_j=S_{j-1}\\cup\\{P_j\\}\\).  \nProceeding up to \\(j=k\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines, each containing a new point of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Covering the remaining points with at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(S\\subseteq T_n\\) be any subset with \\(|S|\\ge k\\).  \nThere exists a collection \\(\\mathcal N\\) of at most \\(n-k\\) distinct lines taken from \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) that covers all points of \\(T_n\\setminus S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet \\(U:=T_n\\setminus S\\).  \nIf \\(U=\\varnothing\\) we are done (take \\(\\mathcal N=\\varnothing\\)).  \nOtherwise, repeat the following operation while \\(U\\neq\\varnothing\\):\n\n* pick the smallest integer \\(c\\) such that a point of \\(U\\) has \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(c\\);\n* add the vertical line \\(x=c\\) to \\(\\mathcal N\\);\n* delete from \\(U\\) every point lying on that line.\n\nEach vertical line contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) (namely \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,2)\\) as long as \\(c\\le n-1\\); for the largest possible \\(c=n\\) it contains the single point \\((n,1)\\), but then the process stops).  \nConsequently each iteration removes at least two points from \\(U\\).  \nSince \\(|U|=|T_n|-|S|\\le |T_n|-k\\) and \\(|T_n|=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2-1\\le n(n+1)/2\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|U|\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nThus after at most \\(n-k\\) iterations the set \\(U\\) becomes empty, and the obtained family \\(\\mathcal N\\) consists of at most \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines, all belonging to \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. By Lemma 2 obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) and let  \n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\{\\,P\\in T_n\\mid P\\text{ lies on some }L_i\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the lemma \\(|S|\\ge k\\).\n\n2. Apply Lemma 3 to the set \\(S\\).  \n   We obtain a family \\(\\mathcal N\\) of at most \\(n-k\\) distinct lines taken from \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) that covers every point of \\(T_n\\setminus S\\).\n\n3. If \\(|\\mathcal N|<n-k\\) we may arbitrarily add unused lines from \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) until the total number of added non‑sunny lines equals exactly \\(n-k\\); this does not affect the covering property.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAny admissible configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\). Hence \\(k>n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nAll and only the integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsatisfy the conditions of the problem.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
