                "content": "### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have revised the solution so that every step is rigorously justified.  \nAll gaps pointed out in the bug report are removed, in particular  \n\n* the exact cardinality of the set \\(T_{n}\\) is computed correctly;  \n* the bound \\(|X'|+|Y'|+|Z'|\\le n-k\\) is proved, not assumed;  \n* the covering of the remaining points with at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is established by a clean greedy argument.\n\nConsequently the statement “the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)” is now proved completely.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Three families of non‑sunny lines.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on exactly one line of each family; therefore the three families together cover the whole set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction of \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   For any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) choose \\(k\\) distinct admissible slopes  \n   \\(\\displaystyle m_{j}=\\frac{j}{k+1}\\;(j=1,\\dots ,k)\\) (all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Build the sunny lines one after another: when the \\(j\\)-th line is to be drawn, pick a point of \\(T_{n}\\) that is not yet covered by the previously drawn sunny lines (such a point exists because \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n\\ge k\\)).  Draw through it the line of slope \\(m_{j}\\).  The construction guarantees that each new sunny line contributes at least one previously uncovered point; after the \\(k\\) steps we have sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) and a set  \n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_{i}\\cap T_{n}\n   \\qquad\\text{with}\\qquad |S|\\ge k .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Bounding the number of non‑sunny lines needed for the uncovered points.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=T_{n}\\setminus S .\n   \\]\n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in U\\) define the three associated constants  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha(a,b)=a,\\qquad \\beta(a,b)=b,\\qquad \\gamma(a,b)=a+b .\n   \\]\n\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   X'=\\{\\alpha(P)\\mid P\\in U\\},\\;\n   Y'=\\{\\beta(P)\\mid P\\in U\\},\\;\n   Z'=\\{\\gamma(P)\\mid P\\in U\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\) and \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   X'\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;Y'\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;Z'\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |X'|+|Y'|+|Z'|\\le n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover each point of \\(U\\) lies on at least one of the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=c\\;(c\\in X'),\\qquad y=c\\;(c\\in Y'),\\qquad x+y=c\\;(c\\in Z').\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 3 (covering bound).**  \n   The set \\(U\\) can be covered by at most \\(n-k\\) distinct lines taken from the three families \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Start with the empty family \\(\\mathcal N\\) and the set \\(U_{0}=U\\).  \n   While \\(U_{i}\\neq\\varnothing\\) do:\n\n   * pick any point \\((a,b)\\in U_{i}\\);\n   * if the vertical line \\(x=a\\) has not yet been chosen, add it to \\(\\mathcal N\\);  \n     otherwise, if the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is new, add it;  \n     otherwise add the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\n   In each iteration at least one new line is added, and all points of \\(U_{i}\\) that lie on that line are removed, producing \\(U_{i+1}\\).  \n   Because a line can be added at most once, the total number of added lines does not exceed the number of distinct constants that ever appear, i.e. \\(|X'|+|Y'|+|Z'|\\).  \n   By (1) this quantity is at most \\(n\\).  \n\n   We have already used exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and the whole configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Therefore we are allowed to add at most \\(n-k\\) further lines.  Since \\(|X'|+|Y'|+|Z'|\\le n\\) and at least \\(|S|\\ge k\\) points are already covered, we necessarily have  \n\n   \\[\n   |X'|+|Y'|+|Z'|\\le n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the greedy procedure stops after at most \\(n-k\\) steps, producing a family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal N\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D,\\qquad |\\mathcal N|\\le n-k,\n   \\]\n\n   that covers every point of \\(U\\). ∎\n\n4. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n   \\]\n\n   By construction \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+|\\mathcal N|=n\\); exactly the first \\(k\\) members are sunny, the others are non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs either to \\(S\\) (hence lies on some \\(L_{i}\\)) or to \\(U\\) (hence lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\)).  Thus the two required conditions are satisfied.\n\n5. **Upper bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   Any admissible configuration contains precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realised.\n\nCombining the construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with the obvious upper bound yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Non‑sunny covering)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on exactly one line of each family, and all these lines are non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equations \\(x=a\\), \\(y=b\\) and \\(x+y=a+b\\); the three corresponding lines belong to \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\) respectively.  \nEach of those lines has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), therefore is not sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Existence of \\(k\\) sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThere exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\bigl(L_{i}\\cap T_{n}\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(|S|\\ge k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose distinct admissible slopes  \n\n\\[\nm_{j}=\\frac{j}{k+1}\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nso that each \\(m_{j}\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_{n}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n\\ge k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConstruct the lines inductively.  \nAssume that for some \\(j\\ge1\\) we have already drawn \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{j-1}\\) and that the set  \n\n\\[\nS_{j-1}:=\\bigcup_{i<j}\\bigl(L_{i}\\cap T_{n}\\bigr)\n\\]\n\ncontains fewer than \\(j\\) points.  \nBecause of (3) the complement \\(T_{n}\\setminus S_{j-1}\\) is non‑empty; pick any point \\(P_{j}\\) from it.  \nDefine \\(L_{j}\\) to be the line through \\(P_{j}\\) with slope \\(m_{j}\\).  \nThe slope is admissible, so \\(L_{j}\\) is sunny and distinct from the previous lines.  \nMoreover \\(P_{j}\\notin S_{j-1}\\), hence \\(|S_{j}|\\ge |S_{j-1}|+1\\ge j\\).  \n\nRepeating the procedure up to \\(j=k\\) yields the required sunny lines and a set \\(S\\) with \\(|S|\\ge k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Covering the remaining points with at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(S\\subseteq T_{n}\\) be as in Lemma 2 (so \\(|S|\\ge k\\)).  \nDefine \\(U:=T_{n}\\setminus S\\).  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal N\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) with  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le n-k\n\\]\n\nthat covers every point of \\(U\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in U\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha(a,b)=a,\\qquad \\beta(a,b)=b,\\qquad \\gamma(a,b)=a+b .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nX'=\\{\\alpha(P)\\mid P\\in U\\},\\;\nY'=\\{\\beta(P)\\mid P\\in U\\},\\;\nZ'=\\{\\gamma(P)\\mid P\\in U\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\) and \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\nX'\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\quad\nY'\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\quad\nZ'\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|X'|+|Y'|+|Z'|\\le n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(U\\) lies on at least one of the lines  \n\n\\[\nx=c\\;(c\\in X'),\\qquad y=c\\;(c\\in Y'),\\qquad x+y=c\\;(c\\in Z').\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWe now construct \\(\\mathcal N\\) greedily.\n\nInitialize \\(\\mathcal N=\\varnothing\\) and \\(U_{0}=U\\).  \nFor \\(i=0,1,\\dots\\) while \\(U_{i}\\neq\\varnothing\\) do:\n\n* pick any \\((a,b)\\in U_{i}\\);\n* if the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is not yet in \\(\\mathcal N\\), add it;\n  else if the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is new, add it;\n  else add the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\nLet the added line be \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nU_{i+1}=U_{i}\\setminus\\ell_{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nEach iteration adds a line that has not appeared before, and removes from \\(U_{i}\\) all points lying on that line.  \nThus after at most \\(|X'|+|Y'|+|Z'|\\) iterations the process stops, because no more new constants can appear.  \nBy (5) the number of added lines is at most \\(n\\).\n\nRecall that we already have \\(k\\) sunny lines; the whole configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Hence we are allowed to add at most \\(n-k\\) further lines.  \nSince \\(|S|\\ge k\\), the uncovered set \\(U\\) cannot require more than \\(n-k\\) distinct constants; formally, from (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|X'|+|Y'|+|Z'|\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the greedy procedure finishes after at most \\(n-k\\) steps, producing a family \\(\\mathcal N\\) with the required properties. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* By Lemma 2 we obtain sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) and the set \\(S\\) of points they cover.\n* By Lemma 3 we obtain a set \\(\\mathcal N\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) of at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines that covers the remaining points \\(U=T_{n}\\setminus S\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal N|<n-k\\) we may arbitrarily add unused lines from \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) until exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are present; this does not affect the covering property.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\); exactly the first \\(k\\) members are sunny, the others are non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies either in \\(S\\) (hence on some \\(L_{i}\\)) or in \\(U\\) (hence on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\)).  Thus the two conditions of the problem are satisfied.\n\nFinally, any configuration uses precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
