            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Non‑sunny covering)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on exactly one line of each family, and all these lines are non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equations \\(x=a\\), \\(y=b\\) and \\(x+y=a+b\\); the three corresponding lines belong to \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\) respectively.  \nEach of those lines has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), therefore is not sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Existence of \\(k\\) sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThere exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\bigl(L_{i}\\cap T_{n}\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\text{satisfies}\\qquad |S|\\ge k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct admissible slopes (any numbers different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nBecause  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n\\ge k ,\n\\]\n\nthe set \\(T_{n}\\) contains at least \\(k\\) points.  \nConstruct the lines inductively.  \nAssume that for some \\(j\\ge1\\) the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{j-1}\\) have already been drawn and that \\(|\\bigcup_{i<j}L_{i}\\cap T_{n}|\\le j-1\\).  \nPick a point \\(P_{j}\\in T_{n}\\) not belonging to any previous line (possible because of the cardinality estimate).  \nDraw through \\(P_{j}\\) the line of the \\(j\\)-th chosen slope; this line is sunny and distinct from the previous ones.  \nSince \\(P_{j}\\) is new, after adding \\(L_{j}\\) we have \\(|\\bigcup_{i\\le j}L_{i}\\cap T_{n}|\\ge j\\).  \nProceed up to \\(j=k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Covering the uncovered points with at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(S\\) be as in Lemma 2 and put \\(U:=T_{n}\\setminus S\\).  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal N\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) with  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le n-k\n\\]\n\nthat covers every point of \\(U\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line of \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (the vertical and horizontal lines contain exactly \\(n\\) points, the diagonal lines contain at most \\(n\\) points).  \nConsequently a collection of \\(m\\) such lines can cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nFrom Lemma 2 we have \\(|S|\\ge k\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n|U|=|T_{n}|-|S|\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe inequality  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-k\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nholds for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) (it simplifies to \\(n\\ge2\\)).  \nThus the budget of \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is, in principle, sufficient to cover all points of \\(U\\).\n\nWe now exhibit an explicit covering that never exceeds this budget.\n\nInitialize \\(\\mathcal N=\\varnothing\\) and \\(U_{0}=U\\).  \nWhile \\(U_{i}\\neq\\varnothing\\) do:\n\n1. Choose any point \\((a,b)\\in U_{i}\\).\n2. If the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is not yet in \\(\\mathcal N\\), add it;  \n   else if the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is new, add it;  \n   else add the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\nDenote the added line by \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nU_{i+1}=U_{i}\\setminus\\ell_{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nEach iteration adds a line that has not appeared before, and removes from the current uncovered set all points lying on that line; therefore \\(|U_{i+1}|<|U_{i}|\\).  \nConsequently the process stops after at most \\(|U|\\) iterations, and the total number of added lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le |U|.\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) and (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le |U|\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2-k\\le (n-k)n,\n\\]\n\nand because each line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, the inequality forces  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nThus the greedy algorithm yields a family \\(\\mathcal N\\) of at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines that covers all points of \\(U\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* By Lemma 2 we obtain sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) and the set \\(S\\) of points they cover, with \\(|S|\\ge k\\).\n* By Lemma 3 we obtain a set \\(\\mathcal N\\) of at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines that covers the remaining points \\(U=T_{n}\\setminus S\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal N|<n-k\\) we may arbitrarily add unused lines from \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) until exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are present; this does not affect the covering property.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\); precisely the first \\(k\\) members are sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies either in \\(S\\) (hence on a sunny line) or in \\(U\\) (hence on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\)).  \nThus the two required conditions are satisfied.\n\nFinally, any admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nTherefore no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realised.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
