Because \\(k\\) is integral, \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Construction for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) consider the line \\(L_{i}\\) passing through the lattice point \\((i,i)\\) with a slope chosen as follows: start from the diagonal line \\(x+y=2i\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) and rotate it by a very small irrational angle \\(\\varepsilon_{i}>0\\).  \n   The rotation is small enough that the line still passes through \\((i,i)\\) (the point is fixed), and the resulting slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n   Distinctness of the slopes guarantees that the \\(L_{i}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   Each \\(L_{i}\\) contains the single point \\((i,i)\\) of \\(T_{n}\\); no other point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on any \\(L_{i}\\) because any other lattice point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\) would give a slope different from the chosen one.\n\n2. **Uncovered points after the sunny lines.**  \n   The set of points still uncovered is  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=T_{n}\\setminus\\{(1,1),\\dots ,(k,k)\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a\\neq b\\text{ or }a>k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   In particular, every point of \\(U\\) satisfies either \\(a\\ge k+1\\) or \\(b\\ge k+1\\).\n\n3. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   We now use exactly \\(n-k\\) lines from the families of Lemma 1.\n\n   *Vertical lines.*  \n   Take the vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x_{\\,n-k}\\).  \n   They cover all points of \\(U\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Horizontal lines.*  \n   If after the vertical lines some points of \\(U\\) remain, then those points have \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(>n-k\\).  \n  