scribed in Step 2.1 (removing the redundant ones so that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines remain).  \n   - If \\(k\\ge 1\\) we replace \\(k\\) of the vertical/horizontal lines by \\(k\\) **sunny** lines, each of which passes through two points of \\(\\Delta_1\\) (or one point of \\(\\Delta_1\\) and one point already covered by the remaining non‑sunny lines).  \n\n   Concretely, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{i}{i+1},\n   \\]\n\n   which has slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq 0,\\,\\infty,\\,-1\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The line \\(S_i\\) contains the two lattice points \\((i,i)\\) and \\((i+1,i-1)\\) (both belong to \\(S_n\\) because \\(i+(i)=2i\\le n+1\\) and \\((i+1)+(i-1)=2i\\le n+1\\) for all \\(i\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\)).  \n   Thus each \\(S_i\\) covers two points of \\(\\Delta_1\\) that were not covered by the remaining non‑sunny lines after we delete the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (or the horizontal line \\(y=i\\)).\n\n   After deleting the \\(k\\) vertical (or horizontal) lines \\(x=i\\) (or \\(y=i\\)) and inserting the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\) we still have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered, and precisely \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n   *Step 2.3 – Verification.*  \n   - **Distinctness.** The slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) are all different, and none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently the \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct and distinct from every remaining vertical, horizontal or the line \\(D\\).  \n   - **Coverage.** Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on one of the unchanged vertical/horizontal lines, on the line \\(D\\), or on some \\(S_i\\) (the latter holds for the points of \\(\\Delta_1\\) that were removed from the vertical/horizontal grid).  \n   - **Number of sunny lines.** Exactly the inserted lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny; all other lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\). Hence the total