-1)+1=2n-1\\) lines, more than the required number \\(n\\).  We shall delete \\(n-1\\) of them and, if necessary, replace the deleted lines by sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Inserting sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{i}{i+1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nEquation (2) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)y = i x + i .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=i\\); substituting \\(x=i+1\\) gives \\(y=i-1\\).  \nConsequently the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,i-1)\n\\]\n\nboth belong to \\(S_n\\) (indeed \\(i+i=2i\\le n+1\\) and \\((i+1)+(i-1)=2i\\le n+1\\) because \\(i\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\le n-1\\)).  \nThus each sunny line \\(S_i\\) passes through the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).\n\nNow delete from the family (1) the vertical lines \\(V_i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) (any other choice of \\(k\\) vertical or horizontal lines would work equally well) and add the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \nThe resulting collection\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{V_i\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{H_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{D\\}\n\\cup\\{S_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1-k)+(n-1)+1+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Verification of the construction\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe slopes of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are:\n- \\(0\\) for the horizontal lines \\(H_j\\);\n- \\(\\infty\\) for the remaining vertical lines \\(V_i\\);\n- \\(-1\\) for \\(D\\);\n- \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) for \\(S_i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)).  \n\nAll these slopes are different, so the lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nOn