            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Necessary condition.**  \n   A family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so \\(k\\le n\\); trivially \\(k\\ge 0\\). Hence only the integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can occur.\n\n2. **Sufficient condition – explicit construction.**  \n   For a fixed \\(n\\) and any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we build a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 2.1 – A non‑sunny covering.*  \n   The vertical lines \\(V_i:x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\), the horizontal lines \\(H_j:y=j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-1)\\) and the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   are respectively parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-axis and to the line \\(x+y=0\\); none of them is sunny.  