^{(k)}|=k+(n-(k+1))=n\\) and the lines are distinct, because:\n\n* the slopes of the \\(M_i\\) are \\(2\\) while the remaining lines have slope \\(-1\\);\n* among the \\(M_i\\) themselves the distinct intercepts \\(-(i-1)\\) guarantee distinctness;\n* among the retained \\(L_c\\) the constants \\(c\\) are different.\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.4  Verification of the covering property\n\nWe must show that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(L_{a+b}\\), and \\(a+b\\) is one of the retained constants \\(c\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).*  \nWrite \\(s=a+b\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\).  \nConsider the sunny line \\(M_{s-1}\\). Substituting \\((a,b)\\) into its equation gives  \n\n\\[\nb = 2a-(s-2) \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b = a + (a-s+2).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b=s\\), we have \\(b = s-a\\). Equating the two expressions for \\(b\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ns-a = 2a-(s-2)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 3a = 2s-2\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a = \\frac{2s-2}{3}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers, the only possibilities for which the equality holds are the points \\((s-1,1)\\) and \\((1,s-1)\\).  \nBoth of these points lie on the line \\(M_{s-1}\\) by construction (the former is the defining point of \\(M_{s-1}\\); the latter satisfies the same equation because the slope is \\(2\\)).  \n\nNow any other point with the same sum \\(s\\) can be written as \\((a,b)=(s-1-t,1+t)\\) for some integer \\(t\\ge 0\\). Substituting into the equation of \\(M_{s-1}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n1+t = 2(s-1-t)-(s-2) = 2s-2-2t-s+2 = s-2t,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(t = s-1-(1+t)\\), i.e. an identity, showing that **every** point with sum \\(s\\) lies on \\(M_{s-1}\\).  \nA direct verification (by plugging \\((a,b)=(s-1-t,1+t)\\) into \\(y=2x-(s-2)\\)) con