elf belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(s\\). The set \\(D_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points, namely  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that \\(\\ell_s\\notin\\mathcal L\\).  \nEach point of \\(D_s\\) must then be covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) different from \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nAny such line is either sunny (slope \\(\\neq -1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) but a different intercept, i.e. it is parallel to \\(\\ell_s\\) and therefore disjoint from \\(D_s\\).  \nHence every line that covers a point of \\(D_s\\) must be sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(D_s\\).  \nConsequently covering all \\(s-1\\) points of \\(D_s\\) would require at least \\(s-1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nNow sum this requirement over all values of \\(s\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\nThus at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) distinct lines would be needed to cover all points of \\(S_n\\) if none of the diagonal lines \\(\\ell_s\\) were present.\n\nBut the problem stipulates that exactly \\(n\\) lines are used, and for \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n .\n\\]\n\nThis contradiction shows that our assumption \\(\\ell_s\\notin\\mathcal L\\) is false; therefore \\(\\ell_s\\in\\mathcal L\\) for every \\(s\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence for the number of sunny lines\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain the \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\), all of which have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore **not** sunny.  \nSince the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\), there is no room for any additional line, sunny or otherwise.\n\nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_