g to each of the three families of forbidden directions. The total number of points that could be covered would then be at most  \n\n\\[\nn+n+n = 3n .\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(3n < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_{n}|\\).  \nThus the covering condition would be violated. Hence at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be non‑sunny, which yields the desired inequality. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Construction)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers \\(m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad m_{i}\\neq m_{j}\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\]  \n\nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in T_{n}\\) (possible because \\(|T_{n}|\\ge k\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\) let \\(L_{i}\\) be the unique line of slope \\(m_{i}\\) passing through \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\).  \nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(L_{i}\\) are distinct; none of them is parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so they are sunny.  \nThe set \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\) covers exactly the \\(k\\) points \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\).\n\n**Remaining points.**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR = T_{n}\\setminus\\{(a_{1},b_{1}),\\dots ,(a_{k},b_{k})\\},\n\\qquad |R| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k .\n\\]  \n\nSince \\(k\\le n-2\\),\n\n\\[\n|R|\\le \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-2)=\\frac{n^{2}-n+4}{2}\\le 2n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n**Two non‑sunny lines covering \\(R\\).**  \nConsider the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consisting of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) and the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \nEvery line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is non‑sunny, and each contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n\nBecause of (2), the set \\(R\\) contains at most \\(2n\\) points.  \nHence we can select a vertical line \\(V:x=c_{V}\\) that contains the largest possible number of points of \\(R\\); let this number be \\(v\\).  \nThe remaining points of \\(R\\) are at most \\(2n-v\\).  \nChoose a diagonal line \\(D:x+y=c_{D}\\) that contains as many of the remaining points as possible; let this number be \\(d\\).  \nSince each diagonal can contain at most \\(n\\) points, we have \\(d\\ge 2n-v-n = n-v\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nv+d \\ge v+(n-v)=n \\ge |R|-n .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|R|\\le 2n\\), the inequality \\(v+d\\ge |R|\\) follows, which means that every point of \\(R\\) lies on either \\(V\\) or \\(D\\).  \nThus \\(V\\) and \\(D\\) together cover all of \\(R\\).  \nThe two lines are distinct (a vertical line cannot be parallel to a line of slope \\(-1\\)) and both are non‑sunny.\n\n**Final family.**  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L = \\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k},V,D\\}\n\\]  \ncontains \\(k+2=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 1 shows that any admissible configuration can have at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
