ly the remaining points of \\(D_s\\).  The set of indices above has cardinality \\(n-k\\); therefore we add exactly \\(n-k\\) such lines, all distinct.\n\n3. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   - If \\(s\\le k+1\\) then \\(|D_s|=s-1\\le k\\); the \\(k\\) sunny lines already cover all points of \\(D_s\\).  \n   - If \\(s\\ge k+2\\) then the sunny lines cover \\(k\\) points of \\(D_s\\) and the line \\(\\ell_s\\) covers the remaining \\((s-1)-k\\) points.  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n4. **Counting lines.**  \n   The construction uses \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(\\ell_s\\), altogether \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Upper bound)\n\nIn any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Trivial, because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemmas 3 and 4 together give the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) the explicit construction in Lemma 3 provides the required set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
