ny line to contain **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Moreover, a sunny line that contains two points of \\(S_n\\) must have slope of the form\n   \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) where \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\) and\n   \\(|a_2-a_1|,|b_2-b_1|\\ge 2\\); such a line cannot be parallel to any other sunny line that also contains two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-t\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2(n-t)\\).\n\nPutting the three observations together we obtain the inequality  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\;\\le\\; t+2(n-t)=2n-t .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) (the number of integer points in the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)),\nthe inequality yields  \n\n\\[\nt\\;\\ge\\;2n-|S_n|=2n-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}= \\frac{4n-n^2+n}{2}= \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\) is an integer and \\(t\\le n\\), the strongest bound we can deduce is  \n\n\\[\nt\\;\\le\\; n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k=n-t\\) is the number of sunny lines, this is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \nThe bound is sharp for all \\(n\\ge 3\\): a construction using exactly \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines together with the remaining non‑sunny lines (each passing through a distinct point of \\(S_n\\)) achieves the required covering, showing that every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nis indeed attainable.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the number of integer points in a right‑angled\nisosceles triangle of side length \\(n\\); a simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=1+2+\\dots +(n-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the\nthree forbidden directions:\nhorizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) and diagonal\n(\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nConsequently a non‑sunny line is parallel to exactly one of these three\ndirections.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Non‑sunny lines cover at most one point of \\(S_n\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is horizontal, all its points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate.\nTwo distinct points of \\(S_n\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates because the\ncondition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\) to be uniquely determined by the pair\n\\((a,b)\\).  The same argument works for vertical lines (distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates)\nand for diagonal lines (distinct values of \\(x+y\\)). ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition.\nDenote by  \n\n* \\(t\\) the number of non‑sunny lines,\n* \\(k=n-t\\) the number of sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma 1 the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(t\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\).  Hence the sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-t \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (A sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most two points)\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line and suppose it contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not horizontal, the slopes between consecutive points are\nequal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}=m .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is not diagonal, we have \\(m\\neq -1\\); since it is not vertical,\n\\(a_2-a_1\\neq0\\).  The equality of the two fractions forces  \n\n\\[\n(b_2-b_1)(a_3-a_2)=(b_3-b_2)(a_2-a_1).\n\\]\n\nAll numbers involved are positive integers.  Reducing modulo \\(2\\) shows that\n\\(a_2-a_1\\) and \\(a_3-a_2