ection so that the\nline does not pass through any other point of \\(S_n\\); this is always possible\nbecause each such direction fixes either the \\(x\\)-, the \\(y\\)- or the\n\\(x+y\\)-coordinate).  These \\(t\\) lines are non‑sunny and each covers exactly\none point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are at most \\(2k_0\\) in number (by (5) they are\nexactly \\(2k_0\\)).  Pair them arbitrarily and for each pair draw the unique line\nthrough the two points; by Lemma 2 this line is sunny.  The resulting \\(k_0\\)\nsunny lines together with the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines cover all points of\n\\(S_n\\), and all lines are distinct because each pair of points determines a\ndifferent line.\n\nThus the bound (5) is attainable, and any integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\ncan be realized by adding extra sunny lines (which may be taken parallel to\neach other but not parallel to the forbidden directions) while keeping the\ncovering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\right\\}} .\n\\]"
