y the vertical line \\(V_{i}\\)??  \n\n   To resolve this we use the following *pair\u2011replacement* operation.\n\n4. **Pair\u2011replacement operation.**  \n   For any \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_i = \\bigl\\{\\,V_j\\mid j\\neq i,i+1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_i\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) lines (the \\(n-2\\) untouched vertical lines and the sunny line \\(S_i\\)).  \n   We claim that \\(\\mathcal L_i\\) still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   \u2013 Points belonging to a column \\(C_j\\) with \\(j\\neq i,i+1\\) are covered by the vertical line \\(V_j\\) which is still present.  \n   \u2013 The point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n   \u2013 The point \\((i+1,2)\\) also lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n   \u2013 For the remaining points of the columns \\(C_i\\) and \\(C_{i+1}\\) (i.e. those with \\(b\\ge 2\\) in \\(C_i\\) and \\(b\\ge 3\\) in \\(C_{i+1}\\)) we observe that they satisfy \\(b\\le n+1-i\\) and \\(b\\le n-i\\) respectively, hence they also satisfy \\(b\\le n+1-j\\) for the same \\(j\\). In particular each such point belongs to the vertical line \\(V_{i}\\) or \\(V_{i+1}\\) **which are missing**; however it also belongs to the vertical line \\(V_{i-1}\\) or \\(V_{i+2}\\) ?  \n\n   The above direct argument is messy; a cleaner approach is to use *two* sunny lines for each pair of consecutive columns.\n\n5. **Two\u2011sunny\u2011line replacement.**  \n   For any \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) consider the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i^{(1)}:\\;y = x-i+1 ,\\qquad\n   S_i^{(2)}:\\;y = x-i+2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Their slopes are \\(1\\); both are sunny.  \n   Their point sets are  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i^{(1)}\\cap T_n = \\{(i,1),(i+1,2)\\},\n   \\qquad\n   S_i^{(2)}\\cap T_n = \\{(i,2),(i+1,3)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   If we delete the three vertical lines \\(V_i, V_{i+1}, V_{i+2}\\) and insert the two sunny lines \\(S_i^{(1)},S_i^{(2)