 with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{S_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{V_j\\mid k+1\\le j\\le n\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe family consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Columns \\(C_j\\) with \\(j\\ge k+1\\).*  \nEach such column is contained in the vertical line \\(V_j\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Column \\(C_i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).*  \nThe point \\((i,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\) by (2.2).  \nLet \\((i,b)\\in C_i\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \nBecause \\((i,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-i\\); hence \\(b-1\\le n-i\\).  \nConsequently the point \\((i+1,b-1)\\) also belongs to \\(T_n\\) and therefore to the column \\(C_{i+1}\\).  \nIf \\(i<k\\) then \\(i+1\\le k\\) and the line \\(V_{i+1}\\) is **not** present; however the point \\((i+1,b-1)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(S_{i+1}\\) when \\(b-1=1\\) (i.e. \\(b=2\\)).  \nIf \\(b\\ge 3\\) then \\(b-1\\ge 2\\) and the point \\((i+1,b-1)\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(V_{i+2}\\), which is present because \\(i+2\\ge k+1\\).  \nThus \\((i,b)\\) is covered in all cases.\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of having \\(k=n\\) sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nSince a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, each line contains at most one point with a given \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(2,1),\\;\\dots ,\\;(n,1),\n\\]\n\nall having distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates.  \nCovering these \\(n\\) points with \\(n\\) non‑vertical lines would require each line to contain exactly one of them.  \nConsequently none of the lines could contain any other point of \\(T_n\\).  \nBut the point \\((1,2)\\in T_n\\) must also be covered, forcing some line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) to contain both \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\), which is impossible because a non‑vertical line cannot contain two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nThis contradiction shows that a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist; therefore the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1}.\n\\]\n\nThe construction in Section 3 provides, for each such \\(k\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, while the argument of Section 4 proves that \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
