 non‑vertical lines forces each line to contain exactly one of them, leaving no line available to cover any other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Yet the point \\((1,2)\\in T_n\\) must also be covered, which is impossible because the line already containing \\((1,1)\\) cannot contain another point with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \n   Hence a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist; the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 5 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable, and step 6 shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\nC_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\;(1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The all‑vertical family ( \\(k=0\\) )\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\) are distinct, none of them is sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and every column \\(C_i\\) is contained in \\(V_i\\).  \nThus \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines of slope 1\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) put  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y = x-i+1 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n\\(S_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); consequently it is sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nA direct substitution shows that the intersection of \\(S_i\\) with the set \\(T_n\\) is\n\n\\[\nS_i\\cap T_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{(i,1),(i+1,2)\\}, & 1\\le i\\le n-2,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{(n-1,1)\\},        & i=n-1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\((i,1)\\) always satisfies \\(1=i-i+1\\) and \\(i+1\\le n\\); the point \\((i+1,2)\\) satisfies \\(2=i+1-i+1\\) and belongs to \\(T_n\\) exactly when \\((i+1)+2\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(i\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  Consider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{S_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{V_j\\mid k+1\\le j\\le n\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe family consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.1 (Coverage)\n\n*For every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe prove by induction on the sum \\(s=a+b\\) of a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n**Base \\(s=2\\).**  \nThe unique point with \\(a+b=2\\) is \\((1,1)\\).  \nIf \\(k\\ge 1\\) then \\((1,1)\\in S_1\\) by (2.2); otherwise \\(k=0\\) and \\(V_1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). Hence \\((1,1)\\) is covered.\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume every point of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(<s\\) is covered, where \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=s\\).\n\n*Case \\(b=1\\).*  \nIf \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,1)\\in S_a\\) by (2.2); if \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then \\(V_a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains \\((a,1)\\). Thus \\((a,1)\\) is covered.\n\n*Case \\(b\\ge 2\\).*  \nConsider \\((a-1,b-1)\\). Its sum is \\(s-2<s\\) and it still belongs to \\(T_n\\) because\n\\[\n(a-1)+(b-1)=a+b-2\\le n-1\\le n+1 .\n\\]\nBy the induction hypothesis \\((a-1,b-1)\\) is covered.\n\nIf \\(a\\le k\\) then the line \\(S_a\\) has equation \\(y=x-a+1\\); adding the vector \\((1,1)\\) to any point on \\(S_a\\) yields another point on the same line. Hence \\((a,b)=(a-1,b-1)+(1,1)\\) also lies on \\(S_a\\) and is covered.\n\nIf \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains every point with abscissa \\(a\\), in particular \\((a,b)\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\nAll possibilities are covered, completing the induction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of having \\(k=n\\) sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny (i.e. non‑vertical) lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nA non‑vertical line can contain at most one point with a given \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains the \\(n\\) points\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1),\n\\]\nall with distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates.  \nTherefore each of the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) must contain exactly one of these points, leaving no line free to cover any other point of \\(T_n\\).  \nBut the point \\((1,2)\\in T_n\\) still has to be covered, which is impossible because the line already containing \\((1,1)\\) cannot contain another point with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nHence a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist; the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1}.\n\\]\n\nThe construction in Section 3 provides, for each such \\(k\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, while the argument of Section 4 proves that \\(k=n\\) is impossible."
