n\n**Inductive step.** Assume every point of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(<s\\) (\\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\)) is covered.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=s\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(V_1\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\)* we treat two sub‑cases.\n\n1. **\\(b=1\\).**  \n   - If \\(a\\le k+1\\) then, by (2)–(3), \\((a,1)\\in S_a\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\ge k+2\\) then \\((a,1)\\in V_a\\).  \n   In either case the point is covered.\n\n2. **\\(b\\ge 2\\).**  \n   The point \\((a-1,b-1)\\) satisfies \\((a-1)+(b-1)=s-2<s\\) and still belongs to \\(T_n\\) because\n   \\[\n   (a-1)+(b-1)=a+b-2\\le n-1\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n   By the induction hypothesis \\((a-1,b-1)\\) is covered.\n\n   - If \\(a\\le k+1\\) then \\((a-1,b-1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) (its equation is \\(y=x-a+2\\)). Adding the vector \\((1,1)\\) stays on the same line, so \\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(S_a\\) and is covered.\n\n   - If \\(a\\ge k+2\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains every point with abscissa \\(a\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, completing the induction. ∎\n\n---\n\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines existed.  \nA non‑vertical line can contain at most one point with a given \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) with distinct abscissae, so each of the \\(n\\) lines would have to contain exactly one of them.  \nConsequently no line would be left to cover the point \\((1,2)\\in T_n\\), a contradiction.  \nHence at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can appear.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1}.\n\\]\n\nThe construction (4) together with Lemma 5 shows that each such \\(k\\) is attainable, and the argument above shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n\n### Verification Tas