\(S_n\\) were collinear on a sunny line, the slope of that line would be the ratio of differences of their coordinates. Because all points of \\(S_n\\) have integer coordinates, the slope would be a rational number with denominator dividing the difference of the \\(x\\)-coordinates. The only slopes that allow three points of the triangular lattice to be collinear are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)). Hence a sunny line cannot contain three such points. ∎\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is vertical, say \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then the admissible \\(y\\)-coordinates satisfy \\(1\\le y\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them. The same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) and for a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\). ∎\n\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{t\\cdot n}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}+\\underbrace{k\\cdot 2}_{\\text{sunny}}\n=tn+2(n-t)=2n+t\\,(n-2).\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(N\\) points must be covered,  \n\n\\[\n2n+t\\,(n-2)\\ge N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{N-2n}{\\,n-2\\,}\n   =\\frac{\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2n}{\\,n-2\\,}\n   =\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nHence the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rceil\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Realising every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt_0:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rceil ,\\qquad\nk_0:=n-t_0 .\n\\]\n\nWe show that for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le k_0\\) there exists a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }t\\