M_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-2}{t-3}\\,x+\\frac{2}{t-3},\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]\n> which passes through the two points \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((2,t-2)\\) of \\(S_n\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- Compute the coordinates of the claimed points on the line (3).  \n  Plug \\(x=1\\): \\(y = \\frac{t-2}{t-3} + \\frac{2}{t-3} = \\frac{t}{t-3}\\). This equals \\(t-1\\) only if \\(\\frac{t}{t-3}=t-1\\), i.e. \\(t = (t-1)(t-3)\\), which is false for all \\(t\\ge4\\).  \n  Plug \\(x=2\\): \\(y = \\frac{2(t-2)}{t-3} + \\frac{2}{t-3} = \\frac{2t-2}{t-3}\\). This equals \\(t-2\\) only if \\(2t-2 = (t-2)(t-3)\\), again false.  \n- In fact the points \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((2,t-2)\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=t\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), not on the line defined by (3). Therefore (3) does **not** pass through the intended points.  \n\n**Consequences:**  \n- The line \\(M_t\\) may be sunny (its slope \\(\\frac{t-2}{t-3}\\) is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(t\\ge4\\)), but it does **not** cover the two points that were previously covered by \\(L_t\\). Hence the covering property is lost.  \n\n**Classification:** Critical Error – the construction of the sunny replacement line is incorrect, breaking the entire existence argument.\n\n### 5. Claim that after replacement the covering property is preserved\n\n> “The points \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((2,t-2)\\) were previously covered by \\(L_t\\); after the replacement they are still covered (by \\(M_t\\)). … Consequently the replacement does not destroy the covering property.”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- As shown above, \\(M_t\\) does **not** contain those points, so they become uncovered unless they are covered by some other line.  \n- The only other lines that could cover \\((1,t-1)\\) are \\(L_1\\) (which covers all points with \\(a=1\\)) – indeed \\((1,t-1)\\) is already covered by \\(L_1\\). However \\((2,t-2)\\) is not on \\(L_1\\) nor on \\(L_2\\) (since \\(b\\neq1\\) unless \\(t=3\\)). It is also not on any other diagonal line because the sum of its coordinates is \\(t\\). Thus after removing \\(L_t\\) the point \\((2,t-2)\\) becomes uncovered.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The argument that all points remain covered is false.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error (the preservation claim is invalid).\n\n### 6. Replacement for \\(t=3\\) (equation (4))\n\n> “Instead we replace \\(L_3\\) by the sunny line  \n> \\[\n> M_3:\\; y=2x-3,\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]\n> which passes through the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) of \\(S_n\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- Evaluate \\(M_3\\) at \\(x=1\\): \\(y = 2(1)-3 = -1\\), not \\(2\\). So \\((1,2)\\) is **not** on \\(M_3\\).  \n- At \\(x=2\\): \\(y = 2(2)-3 = 1\\), which **is** the point \\((2,1)\\). Thus only one of the two required points lies on \\(M_3\\). Consequently the point \\((1,2)\\) would be uncovered after the replacement (though it is still covered by \\(L_1\\), but the intended symmetry of the construction fails).  \n\n**Classification:** Justification Gap (the line does not satisfy the claimed property; the argument that the covering is preserved is incomplete).\n\n### 7. Final conclusion\n\n> “By performing the replacement … we obtain a family of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines … Since there are \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines … we can realise any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- Because the replacement step is incorrect, the existence of configurations with the claimed numbers of sunny lines is not established. The conclusion does not follow from the preceding (flawed) construction.\n\n**Classification:** The conclusion rests on critical errors; therefore the overall solution is invalid.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains several critical errors: an incorrect combinatorial bound based on a false statement about sunny lines, and a fundamentally flawed construction that does not actually preserve coverage of the required lattice points. Consequently the claimed set of admissible \\(k\\) values is not rigorously established, and the solution must be rejected."
