ost one point of \\(C_i\\); therefore at least \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\) distinct non‑vertical lines are needed to cover \\(C_i\\).  \nSumming over all \\(i\\) for which the vertical line is missing gives a lower bound on the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in S}(n+1-i)\\ge \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(n+1-i)=T_n>\\!n\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(S\\) is the set of indices whose vertical line is omitted.  \nThus a covering with at most \\(n\\) lines is impossible unless **every** vertical line \\(x=i\\) ( \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) ) is present. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 1 (no sunny line can appear)\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two conditions of the problem.  \nThen every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny; i.e. the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines equals \\(0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 2 the family must contain the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non‑sunny.  \nConsequently only one further line \\(L\\) may be added, because the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\).\n\nThe points not already lying on a vertical line are those with first coordinate \\(a=n\\).  \nThe inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); hence the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).\n\nTherefore the extra line \\(L\\) must pass through \\((n,1)\\).  \nA line through \\((n,1)\\) is\n\n* vertical – impossible because the only vertical line with that \\(x\\)-coordinate would be \\(x=n\\), which is not among the required vertical lines (they stop at \\(x=n-1\\));\n* horizontal, i.e. \\(y=1\\);\n* of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n* or of some other slope (sunny).\n\nBoth the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) are non‑sunny.  \nIf \\(L\\) had any other slope, it would be sunny, but then it would be the **only** sunny line in the whole family.  \nNevertheless, the family would still contain the