sider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1),\\qquad L_n:\\;y=1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) by \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); since \\(b\\ge1\\) we obtain \\(b=1\\), and \\((n,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_n\\).\n\nThus the lines (1) cover the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Introducing sunny lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSelect the \\(k\\) points  \n\n\\[\nQ_j=(j,1)\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhich all belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\(j+1\\le n+1\\).\n\nFor each \\(j\\) choose a real number \\(\\alpha_j\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_j\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y-1=\\alpha_j\\,(x-j) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_j\\) passes through \\(Q_j\\), has slope \\(\\alpha_j\\), and therefore is sunny.  \nBecause the abscissae \\(j\\) are distinct and the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Completing the family  \n\nRemove from the family (1) the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\).  \nThe remaining non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhich are exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct lines.\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines; precisely the first \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(\\ma