neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y-1=\\alpha_j\\,(x-j) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_j\\) passes through \\(Q_j\\), has slope \\(\\alpha_j\\), and therefore is sunny.  \nBecause the abscissae \\(j\\) are distinct and the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Completing the family  \n\nRemove from the family (1) the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\).  \nThe remaining non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhich are exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct lines.\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines; precisely the first \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le a\\le k\\):*  \nThe point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).  \nIndeed, \\(S_a\\) has equation \\(y-1=\\alpha_a(x-a)\\); substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1\\).  \nSince \\(\\alpha_a\\neq0\\), the line is not horizontal, so it also contains every point with the same abscissa \\(a\\), in particular \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(k+1\\le a\\le n-1\\):*  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in (4)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(a=n\\):*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (present in (4)).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Exhaustion of possible values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem requires exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nOur construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); consequently all these values are attainable.  \nNo other value of \\(k\\) can occur because \\(k\\) is by definition a non‑negative integer not exceeding the total number of lines, which is \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Final statement  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
