on‑sunny. By Lemma 2 they cover every column \\(C_a\\) (\\(a\\le n-1\\)), and by Lemma 3 the line \\(y=1\\) covers the remaining point \\((n,1)\\). Hence the family (★) satisfies both conditions of the problem and contains **no** sunny line, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of any sunny line (\\(k\\ge1\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least one sunny line. By Lemma 2 the family must still contain the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\). That already accounts for \\(n-1\\) lines; the remaining line would be the sunny one. However, the point \\((n,1)\\) is not on any of the vertical lines, so the sunny line must pass through \\((n,1)\\). A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it is neither horizontal nor the line \\(x+y=n+1\\). Such a line meets the vertical line \\(x=n\\) only at \\((n,1)\\) and does **not** contain any other point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). Thus all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are already covered by the vertical lines, and the extra sunny line is superfluous. But the problem requires *exactly* \\(n\\) distinct lines; we already have \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, so the last line must be present, and it may be any line through \\((n,1)\\). Choosing a sunny line would be permissible only if it does not violate any other condition, which it does not. Hence at first sight a sunny line seems possible.\n\nThe subtlety is that the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) already cover **all** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) **except** \\((n,1)\\). The point \\((n,1)\\) can be covered by a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny) **or** by the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\), also non‑sunny). If we replace the horizontal line by a sunny line, the family still satisfies the covering condition. However, the definition of a *sunny* line excludes slope \\(-1\\) but allows any other slope, so a sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) is admissible.\n\nThus the previous argument does **not** forbid the existence of a sunny line; the only obstruction is the requirement that the total number of lines be exactly \\(n\\). Since we already have \\(n-1\\) forced vertical lines, we are free to choose **any** line for the last slot, sunny or not. Consequently the earlier conclusion that \\(k\\) must be \\(0\\) is **incorrect**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Correct determination of all possible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Lemma 2 we know that the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) must be present in every admissible family. The remaining line can be **any** line that passes through the uncovered point \\((n,1)\\). There are three mutually exclusive possibilities for its slope:\n\n1. slope \\(0\\) → the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny);\n2. slope \\(-1\\) → the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny);\n3. any other real slope → a sunny line.\n\nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines can be either \\(0\\) (if we choose case 1 or 2) or \\(1\\) (if we choose case 3). No larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because only one line is not forced to be vertical.\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final verification of coverage for both cases  \n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the family (★). As argued above, every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on one of the listed lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nChoose the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and, for the remaining line, any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y-1 = \\alpha (x-n),\\qquad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe vertical lines cover all points with abscissa \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\). Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.\n\nBoth families consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, as required. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
