            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n\nIf a line contains two distinct points of the same column  \n\n\\[\nC_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\},\n\\]\n\nthen the line is vertical (hence non‑sunny).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo points of \\(C_c\\) have the same abscissa \\(c\\); the line through them has undefined slope, i.e. it is the vertical line \\(x=c\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k\n\\tag{V}\n\\]\n\ncover all points of the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n-k\\)) contains exactly the points of the column \\(C_c\\). The columns are disjoint, so the union of the lines in (V) covers precisely the union of those columns. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_{n,k}= \\bigcup_{c=n-k+1}^{n} C_c .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal R_{n,k}\\) contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\) points and can be partitioned into \\(k\\) blocks  \n\n\\[\nB_1,\\dots ,B_k,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(|B_j|=j\\) for each \\(j\\) and the points of each block have distinct abscissae.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(c=n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\) the column \\(C_c\\) has height \\(n+1-c\\); summing these heights gives (1).  \nArrange the points of \\(\\mathcal R_{n,k}\\) in the order  \n\n\\[\n(n,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,3),\\;(n,3),\\dots\n\\]\n\ni.e. for each integer \\(t\\) from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) we list the points whose second coordinate equals \\(t\\) and whose first coordinate runs from \\(n-t+1\\) up to \\(n\\).  \nTaking the first \\(1\\) point gives \\(B_1\\), the next \\(2\\) points give \\(B_2\\), etc.; by construction the abscissae inside a block are all different. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4  \n\nFor any block \\(B_j\\) described in Lemma 3 there exists a sunny line that contains **all** points of \\(B_j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(|B_j|=1\\) we may choose any line through that single point whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line is sunny.  \nIf \\(|B_j|\\ge2\\), pick the first two points of the block, say \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) with \\(x_1\\neq x_2\\). Their slope is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the points of a block have distinct abscissae, the denominator is non‑zero, so the line is not vertical. The points also have distinct ordinates (they belong to the same diagonal \\(y\\)‑level), hence the numerator is non‑zero, so the line is not horizontal. Finally, the difference \\(y_2-y_1\\) equals the difference of the corresponding column indices, which is never equal to \\(-(x_2-x_1)\\); consequently \\(m\\neq -1\\). Hence the line determined by the two points is sunny. By construction every other point of the block lies on the same line (they share the same linear relation), so the whole block is contained in that sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a given \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (V). By Lemma 2 they cover all points of the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   Apply Lemma 3 to the remaining set \\(\\mathcal R_{n,k}\\) and obtain the blocks \\(B_1,\\dots ,B_k\\).  \n   For each \\(j\\) use Lemma 4 to choose a sunny line \\(L_j\\) that contains the whole block \\(B_j\\).\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines (the vertical lines are distinct, and the sunny lines are distinct because they contain points with different abscissae), covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (vertical lines cover the first \\(n-k\\) columns, the sunny lines cover the rest), and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Exhaustiveness\n\nObviously we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total. The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
