"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to determine the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nHowever, I have proved the following rigorous partial result.\n\n> **Partial theorem.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\). If a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition of the problem, then at most \\(n-2\\) of the lines can be sunny. Consequently every admissible integer \\(k\\) satisfies  \n> \\[\n> 0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n> \\]\n\nThe proof shows that at least two of the lines must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n1. **Geometric configuration of the required points.**  \n   The points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) form a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of side length \\(n\\).  \n2. **Counting argument for lines of each direction.**  \n   Any line not parallel to the three forbidden directions meets the lattice in at most one point, because two distinct points of the lattice determine a line whose slope is either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n3. **Consequences.**  \n   Hence every sunny line can cover **at most one** of the required points, while a line parallel to one of the forbidden directions can contain many of them.  \n4. **Lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   There are exactly \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points to be covered. If \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, they can cover at most \\(k\\) points. The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines must therefore cover at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\) points.  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (resp. \\(y\\)-axis, \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) (resp. \\(n\\), \\(n\\)) of the required points. Thus each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   Consequently  \n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,n \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k .\n   \\]\n   Simplifying gives \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that have to lie on the given family of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n} (n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Sunny lines cover at most one required point  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\nTake two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_i,b_i\\ge 1\\) and\n\\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\). Their slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\in\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause the differences \\(b_2-b_1\\) and \\(a_2-a_1\\) are integers of absolute\nvalue at most \\(n\\) and the condition \\(a_1+b_1\\le n+1,\\;a_2+b_2\\le n+1\\)\nforces the numerator and denominator to be either equal, opposite, or one of\nthem zero. Hence any line that contains **two** points of \\(T_n\\) must be\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nTherefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non\u2011sunny lines can contain at most \\(n\\) points  \n\nConsider a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, i.e. of the form \\(y=c\\) with\n\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b=c\\) gives\n\\(a\\le n+1-c\\). Since also \\(a\\ge 1\\), the possible values of \\(a\\) are the\nintegers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them because\n\\(c\\ge 1\\). An analogous argument holds for a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.\nFor a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) we write it as \\(x+y=d\\) with integer\n\\(d\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a+b=d\\) we obtain \\(d\\le n+1\\); together with\n\\(a,b\\ge 1\\) we get \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\) and similarly for \\(b\\). Hence at most\n\\(d-1\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) lie on such a line.  \n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\ncovering condition. Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\nBy the result of \u00a71 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of\n\\(T_n\\). The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, and by \u00a72 each of them\ncovers at most \\(n\\) points. Consequently the total number of covered points\nsatisfies  \n\n\\[\nk + (n-k)\\,n \\;\\ge\\; |T_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n(n-k)n \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nn^2 - kn \\ge \\frac{n^2+n}{2}-k,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n2n^2 - 2kn \\ge n^2 + n - 2k,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nn^2 - 2kn + 2k \\ge n,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n2k \\ge 2kn - n^2 + n,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk \\le n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is a non\u2011negative integer, the set of possible values is contained\nin \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Existence for the values \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (partial)\n\nThe inequality above is necessary. Constructing families that attain every\n\\(k\\) in the range \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) is left open; however, the bound shows\nthat no configuration can have \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines. Hence the problem\nis reduced to exhibiting, for each \\(k\\le n-2\\), a concrete arrangement of\n\\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nAny family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least two lines parallel to one\nof the three forbidden directions. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines can never exceed \\(n-2\\). Determining whether every integer\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) actually occurs remains an open sub\u2011problem."
