ral position with respect to the three forbidden directions, such a choice is always possible; a simple greedy algorithm works: start with the smallest uncovered point of \\(S_{1}\\) (in lexicographic order), pick a slope not used before, and choose the unique line of that slope through the point. This line cannot be parallel to the axes nor to \\(x+y=0\\), hence it is sunny, and it covers at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{1}\\). Repeating this procedure we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that together cover all of \\(S_{1}\\) (the number of points of \\(S_{1}\\) is at most \\(k\\cdot\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) because \\(k\\le n\\)).  \n\n   - The three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\) together cover all points of \\(S_{0}\\).  \n     If \\(n-k>3\\) we add any \\(n-k-3\\) further distinct non‑sunny lines (for instance, extra vertical lines \\(x=2,3,\\dots\\) that do not meet any uncovered point). These extra lines are harmless because they do not introduce new sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every lattice point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n3. **Verification of distinctness** – The construction guarantees that all chosen lines have different slopes or different intercepts, hence they are pairwise distinct.\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All admissible }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
