f lines is\n   \\[\n   (n-2-(k))\\;+\\;2\\;+\\;k = n,\n   \\]\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  By the argument above every point of \\(S\\) remains covered.\n\nThus for each \\(k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Conclusion.*  \nThe upper bound of Step\u202f2 and the constructive lower bound of Step\u202f3 together show that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nHence a non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or diagonal \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Upper bound).  \nIn any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the collection contain \\(h\\) horizontal, \\(v\\) vertical and \\(d\\) diagonal lines, and \\(s\\) sunny lines, so that  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d+s=n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (namely \\((1,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\)).  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line and for a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (the points are \\((1,c-1),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\)).  \nA sunny line has slope different from the three forbidden slopes; consequently it can intersect the integer lattice in at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\). Indeed, if a sunny line contained \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(S_n\\) then those points would have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) (or distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates), forcing the line to be vertical or horizontal, a contradiction.\n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nh\\cdot n+v\\cdot n+d\\cdot n+s\\cdot (n-1)\n   = n(h+v+d)+s(n-1)\n   = n(n-s)+s(n-1)=n^2-s .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^2-s\\) would be impossible, we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2-s\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad s\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Base configuration, \\(k=0\\)).  \nThe following \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) and contain no sunny line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2,\\;x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le 2\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n* If \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\), again on \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  All lines are horizontal or diagonal, hence non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Replacing a horizontal line by a sunny line).  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=-\\frac{j}{n}\\,x+(j+1).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(L_j\\) is sunny and the set of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on the horizontal line \\(y=j+1\\) is exactly the set of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(L_j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope \\(-\\frac{j}{n}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L_j\\) is sunny.  \n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(y=j+1\\) iff \\(b=j+1\\).  \nIt lies on \\(L_j\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb=-\\frac{j}{n}a+(j+1)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a=\\frac{n}{j}(j+1-b).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b=j+1\\) gives \\(a=0\\), the only integer solutions with \\(a\\ge 1\\) occur when \\(b=j+1\\) and \\(a\\) is arbitrary; conversely, if \\(b=j+1\\) the equation reduces to \\(a\\) arbitrary, so the two lines intersect \\(S_n\\) in the same set of lattice points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the base configuration \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of Lemma\u202f2.  \nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) replace the horizontal line \\(y=j+1\\) by the sunny line \\(L_j\\) of Lemma\u202f3.  \nBecause of Lemma\u202f3 the covering property is preserved after each replacement.  \n\nAfter the \\(k\\) replacements we have:\n\n* the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\);\n* the remaining horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) for \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) (there are \\(n-2-k\\) of them);\n* the two diagonal lines \\(x+y=n\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-2-k)+2=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f4 shows that for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.  \nHence the admissible values are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the answer is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).**"
