uld be parallel to a side.  \nTherefore a sunny line misses at least one of the \\(n\\) possible rows and can meet at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1(i) each non‑sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); by Lemma 1(ii) each sunny line covers at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le (n-k)\\,n+k\\,(n-1)=n^{2}-k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-k\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe first give a covering with **no** sunny lines.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nand let \\(\\mathcal F_0=\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\).  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge n-1\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le 2\\).  \n  Consequently \\(a+b=n\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of the two diagonal lines in \\(\\mathcal D\\).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nNow we show how to replace a horizontal line by a sunny one without destroying the covering.\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) and define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=-2x+(2t+1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope 