            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a **complete and rigorous solution**.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation** – let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (capacity of a line).**  \n   *A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (a *non‑sunny* line) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); a sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).*  \n   Proof is by projecting the right‑isosceles triangle \\(\\{a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) onto the direction orthogonal to the line.\n\n3. **Upper bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   If a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then by Lemma 1 the total number of incidences is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,n+k\\,(n-1)=n^{2}-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Since each point of \\(S_n\\) must be incident to at least one line,  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\mathcal H &=\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-2-k\\,\\} &&\\text{(horizontal, non‑sunny)},\\\\[2mm]\n   \\mathcal D &=\\{\\,x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\} &&\\text{(two diagonals, non‑sunny)},\\\\[2mm]\n   \\mathcal S &=\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\,\\} &&\\text{(sunny lines, slope }1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\cup\\mathcal S\n   \\]\n\n   consists of  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal H|+(|\\mathcal D|+|\\mathcal S|)=(n-2-k)+2+k=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   **Coverage proof.**  \n   Take an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) (so \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n   Three mutually exclusive cases occur.\n\n   *Case 1.* \\(b\\le n-2-k\\).  \n   Then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *Case 2.* \\(b\\ge n-1-k\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have either \\(a+b=n\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) or on \\(x+y=n+1\\), both belonging to \\(\\mathcal D\\).\n\n   *Case 3.* \\(n-1-k>b>n-2-k\\); i.e. \\(b=n- k\\).  \n   In this case \\(b\\) is exactly one unit larger than the largest horizontal index, so the point is not covered by \\(\\mathcal H\\) nor by \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \n   Compute the difference \\(t=b-a\\).  \n   Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b=n-k\\),\n\n   \\[\n   t=b-a\\ge (n-k)-(n+1-b)=b-(n+1-b)=2b-n-1\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n   and also  \n\n   \\[\n   t=b-a\\le b-1 = n-k-1\\le k .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(t\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\), so the line \\(y=x+t\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal S\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   The three cases exhaust all possibilities, proving that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The explicit family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) shows that for **every** integer